  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2021-05-22T02:14:42.084Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "If auto-footsteps is unchecked, moving side to side will cause the body to become misaligned with the head when locomotion is stopped. The body will point slightly in the direction of movement after stopping, unless you move back in the other direction to compensate.\nThis issue does not exist if auto-footsteps is enabled. The first image below is with auto-footsteps off, after moving to the side and stopping. The second image is the same with auto-footsteps on.\nI have found that setting hip tracking control to animation while moving also prevents this issue (even with auto-footsteps off.) This is the case even in desktop mode and 3-point VR where hip tracking should not really be a factor, as far as I understand it. I don't have full body tracking and so I cannot test any of this in full body.\n\nEdit: To be more specific, it seems that setting hip tracking to animation then back to tracking is what allows the body orientation to reset, even if standing still. Including a GIF of that from a comment reply below: https://gfycat.com/tediousscentedcaribou\n \nThe hip tracking solution is not desirable for a variety of reasons, but hopefully that detail helps narrow down the issue. The problem occurs with and without write defaults, with both default and custom locomotion animations, in desktop mode and 3-point VR, and with various different humanoid avatars that I have tried.\nI have also recorded a short video demonstrating the issue: https://youtu.be/pRRhMJrpjmg",
